\title{Seed-Compressible Data: Detection, Identification and\\
Exact Seed Recovery for Pseudo-Random Streams}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{17 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A substantial amount of real-world data is not merely \emph{random-looking} but
literally the output of a deterministic pseudo-random generator: procedurally
generated game content, simulation traces, synthetic benchmark corpora,
scrambled transport streams. Such data is statistically incompressible yet has
description complexity equal to that of its seed. We develop the exact
mathematics of exploiting this: detection, identification, and lossless seed
recovery with a bit-exact replay guarantee.

We work with streams over a commutative ring obeying a linear recurrence
$x_{n+L}=\sum_{i<L}c_i x_{n+i}$, the model underlying linear feedback shift
registers, and prove five groups of results. (1) \emph{Exact replay}: a stream
obeying an order-$L$ recurrence is reproduced bit for bit by rerunning the
register from its own first $L$ symbols. (2) \emph{Sample complexity}: via the
$F[X]$-module structure induced by the shift operator, a stream obeys a
recurrence exactly when the characteristic polynomial annihilates it; every
monic polynomial is realized as a characteristic polynomial; linear complexity
is subadditive; hence two streams of complexity at most $L$ agreeing on their
first $2L$ symbols agree identically, and $2L$ cannot be reduced. (3)
\emph{Well-posedness}: tap recovery has a unique answer if and only if the
state windows of the stream span $F^{L}$. (4) \emph{Family collapse}: every
linear congruential stream $x_{n+1}=ax_n+b$ obeys the order-$2$ recurrence with
taps $(-a,1+a)$; seeds are recovered exactly backward by modular inversion and
forward by pure periodicity. (5) \emph{Census and limits}: at most
$4^{L}-2^{L}+1$ files of any length are $L$-seed compressible, a $2^{2L-N}$
fraction of length-$N$ files, and for any fixed decompressor there are files
that are neither seed-compressible nor model-compressible. An exact census of
binary streams of complexity at most $L$ for $L\le 8$ matches
$\tfrac13(2\cdot 4^{L}+1)$ in every case; this is stated as a conjecture.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} linear feedback shift register, linear complexity,
Berlekamp--Massey, seed recovery, linear congruential generator, shift
operator, Hankel rank, description complexity, lossless compression.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The phenomenon}

Compression exploits structure. Classical compressors exploit \emph{statistical}
structure---repeated substrings, skewed symbol frequencies, local
correlation---and are defeated by data whose statistics are flat. But flat
statistics do not imply high information content. The output of a well-chosen
shift register of $32$ cells passes standard randomness tests while being
determined by $64$ bits. A megabyte of such data has description complexity $64$
bits and statistical compressibility zero.

Such data is not rare in the wild. Procedural game worlds are generated from
seeds and stored as assets; simulation studies persist their random streams for
reproducibility; synthetic corpora are generated to benchmark databases;
transport-layer scramblers XOR real payloads with register output. Whenever the
generator and the seed are recoverable, the correct compressed representation
of such a file is the seed.

This paper develops the mathematics of that pipeline: \emph{fingerprint} (which
generator family?), \emph{identify} (which parameters?), \emph{recover} (what
seed?), \emph{verify} (does replay reproduce the file exactly?), and
\emph{bound} (how much data can possibly be handled this way?).

\subsection{Structure}

Section~\ref{sec:model} fixes the model. Section~\ref{sec:replay} proves exact
replay. Section~\ref{sec:2L} develops the polynomial-module machinery and the
$2L$ theorem. Section~\ref{sec:unique} characterizes well-posedness.
Section~\ref{sec:lcg} treats congruential generators. Section~\ref{sec:census}
quantifies the population of seed-compressible files and proves the router
dichotomy. Sections~\ref{sec:algorithms}--\ref{sec:discussion} give algorithms,
applications, and future work.

Throughout, the falsifiability standard is absolute: a compression claim is
accepted only when the decoder, run on the claimed program, reproduces the file
\textbf{bit for bit}.

\section{The model}\label{sec:model}

Let $F$ be a commutative ring (specialized to a field, and often to $\GF(2)$,
where noted). A \emph{stream} is a function $x:\NN\to F$, written $x_n$.

\begin{definition}[Linear recurrence / LFSR stream]
For $L\in\NN$ and a \emph{tap vector} $c=(c_0,\dots,c_{L-1})\in F^{L}$, the
stream $x$ \emph{obeys the order-$L$ recurrence with taps $c$} if
\[
  x_{n+L}\;=\;\sum_{i=0}^{L-1}c_i\,x_{n+i}\qquad\text{for all }n\in\NN .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The generator]
Given taps $c$ and a \emph{seed} $s\in F^{L}$, the register output
$R(c,s):\NN\to F$ is defined by strong recursion:
\[
  R(c,s)_n=\begin{cases}
    s_n, & n<L,\\[2pt]
    \displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{L-1}c_i\,R(c,s)_{n-L+i}, & n\ge L .
  \end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

The recursion is well founded because $n-L+i<n$ whenever $i<L$ and $n\ge L$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:genworks}
$R(c,s)$ obeys the order-$L$ recurrence with taps $c$, and $R(c,s)_n=s_n$ for
$n<L$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For $n\ge L$ the defining clause is the recurrence re-indexed by $n\mapsto n+L$;
the initial clause is the second claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[State window]
The \emph{window} of $x$ at time $n$ is
$w_n(x)=(x_n,x_{n+1},\dots,x_{n+L-1})\in F^{L}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Linear complexity]
A stream $x$ has \emph{complexity at most $L$} if some tap vector $c\in F^{L}$
generates it; the \emph{linear complexity} $\lc(x)$ is the least such $L$
(infinite if none exists).
\end{definition}

The property is upward closed: if $c$ generates $x$ at order $L$ then
$(0,c_0,\dots,c_{L-1})$ generates $x$ at order $L+1$, because the order-$L$
recurrence at index $n+1$ is the order-$(L+1)$ recurrence at index $n$. So
``complexity at most $L$'' and $\lc(x)\le L$ are interchangeable.

\section{Exact replay: the compression guarantee}\label{sec:replay}

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity]\label{thm:rigidity}
Let $x$ and $y$ both obey the order-$L$ recurrence with the \emph{same} taps
$c$, and suppose $x_i=y_i$ for all $i<L$. Then $x=y$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Strong induction on $n$. For $n<L$ this is the hypothesis. For $n\ge L$ write
$n=m+L$; then $x_{m+L}=\sum_i c_i x_{m+i}$ and $y_{m+L}=\sum_i c_i y_{m+i}$, and
every index $m+i$ with $i<L$ is smaller than $n$, so the inductive hypothesis
makes the two sums equal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact replay; the seed-recovery gate]\label{thm:replay}
If $x$ obeys the order-$L$ recurrence with taps $c$, then
\[
  x \;=\; R\bigl(c,\ (x_0,x_1,\dots,x_{L-1})\bigr).
\]
The pair (taps, first $L$ symbols) is a \emph{lossless} representation of the
entire infinite stream.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both sides obey the order-$L$ recurrence with taps $c$
(hypothesis and Proposition~\ref{prop:genworks}) and agree on indices $<L$;
apply Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Zero-seed degeneracy]\label{prop:zeroseed}
For every tap vector $c$ we have $R(c,0)=0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Strong induction: below $L$ the output is the zero seed; above, it is a linear
combination of earlier outputs, all zero by hypothesis.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:zeroseed} is the germ of two later results: it obstructs
uniqueness of the taps (Section~\ref{sec:unique}) and makes the naive parameter
count of seed-compressible files non-tight (Section~\ref{sec:census}).

\subsection{Periodic data as register output}

\begin{definition}
For $p\ge1$, the \emph{recirculating taps} are $u^{(p)}=(1,0,\dots,0)\in F^{p}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Periodicity is a linear recurrence]\label{thm:periodic}
For $p\ge1$, a stream $x$ obeys the order-$p$ recurrence with taps $u^{(p)}$ if
and only if $x_{n+p}=x_n$ for all $n$. Moreover $R(u^{(p)},s)_n=s_{n\bmod p}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The sum $\sum_{i<p}u^{(p)}_i x_{n+i}$ collapses to the term $i=0$, i.e.\ to
$x_n$, so the recurrence reads $x_{n+p}=x_n$. The formula for $R$ follows by
strong induction using $n\bmod p=(n-p)\bmod p$ for $n\ge p$.
\end{proof}

Thus every $p$-periodic file is seed compressible with a $2p$-symbol
description: padding regions, fill patterns, repeated headers and tiled texture
data all fall inside the detector's reach.

\section{Sample complexity: \texorpdfstring{$2L$}{2L} symbols suffice, and no
fewer}\label{sec:2L}

\subsection{Streams as a module over the polynomial ring}

\begin{definition}[Shift operator]
$S:(\NN\to F)\to(\NN\to F)$, $(Sx)_n=x_{n+1}$.
\end{definition}

$S$ is $F$-linear and $(S^{k}x)_n=x_{n+k}$ by induction on $k$; evaluation at
$S$ therefore makes streams a module over $F[X]$ with $X$ acting as $S$:
for $r=\sum_k r_kX^{k}$,
\[
  \bigl(r(S)x\bigr)_n=\sum_k r_k\,x_{n+k}.
\]

\begin{definition}[Characteristic polynomial]
The order-$L$ recurrence with taps $c$ has characteristic polynomial
$\chi_c(X)=X^{L}-\sum_{i=0}^{L-1}c_iX^{i}$, monic of degree $L$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Residual formula]\label{lem:residual}
For every stream $x$ and index $n$,
$\bigl(\chi_c(S)x\bigr)_n=x_{n+L}-\sum_{i<L}c_i x_{n+i}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from $(S^{k}x)_n=x_{n+k}$ and linearity of $r\mapsto r(S)$ applied
termwise to $\chi_c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Annihilation criterion]\label{thm:annihilation}
A stream $x$ obeys the order-$L$ recurrence with taps $c$ if and only if
$\chi_c(S)x=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both statements assert vanishing of the residual of Lemma~\ref{lem:residual} at
every index.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Every monic polynomial is characteristic]\label{thm:realize}
Let $r\in F[X]$ be monic of degree $m$ and define the order-$m$ tap vector by
$c^{(r)}_i=-r_i$ (minus the degree-$i$ coefficient), $i<m$. Then
$\chi_{c^{(r)}}=r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compare coefficients of $X^{k}$ in
$\chi_{c^{(r)}}=X^{m}+\sum_{i<m}r_iX^{i}$. For $k<m$ only the sum contributes,
giving $r_k$; for $k=m$ the leading monomial gives $1=r_m$ by monicity; for
$k>m$ both sides vanish since $\deg r=m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:dictionary}
If a monic $r$ of degree $m$ satisfies $r(S)z=0$ then $\lc(z)\le m$.
Conversely, over a nontrivial ring a stream of complexity at most $L$ is
annihilated by a monic degree-$L$ polynomial, namely the characteristic
polynomial of any generating tap vector.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Subadditivity}

\begin{theorem}[Subadditivity of linear complexity]\label{thm:subadd}
Suppose $F$ has no zero divisors. If $\lc(x)\le L$ and $\lc(y)\le M$ then $\lc(x\pm y)\le L+M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose monic annihilators $p$ of $x$ (degree $L$) and $q$ of $y$ (degree $M$),
which exist by Corollary~\ref{cor:dictionary}. The product $pq$ is monic of
degree $L+M$ because degrees add in the absence of zero divisors. Since $F[X]$
is commutative and the action is by algebra evaluation,
$(pq)(S)x=q(S)\bigl(p(S)x\bigr)=0$ and $(pq)(S)y=p(S)\bigl(q(S)y\bigr)=0$;
hence $(pq)(S)(x+y)=0$ and Corollary~\ref{cor:dictionary} applies. For the
difference, $-y$ has the same annihilators as $y$.
\end{proof}

Operationally: mixing two register streams cannot hide them. A superposition of
a complexity-$L$ and a complexity-$M$ source is detectable by a search up to
order $L+M$.

\subsection{The \texorpdfstring{$2L$}{2L} theorem}

\begin{lemma}[Vanishing initial segment]\label{lem:vanish}
If $\lc(z)\le m$ and $z_i=0$ for all $i<m$, then $z=0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The zero stream obeys every recurrence, so $z$ and $0$ obey the same order-$m$
recurrence and agree below $m$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$2L$ samples determine the stream]\label{thm:2L}
Let $F$ be a nontrivial ring without zero divisors. If $\lc(x)\le L$, $\lc(y)\le L$ and $x_i=y_i$ for all $i<2L$, then $x=y$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:subadd}, $z=x-y$ satisfies $\lc(z)\le 2L$; by
hypothesis $z_i=0$ for $i<2L$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:vanish}, $z=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Detector soundness]\label{cor:sound}
If a file is truly generated by some order-$L$ register and a candidate order-$L$
register reproduces its first $2L$ symbols, then the candidate reproduces the
whole file. Fitting on a $2L$-symbol window is a proof of global agreement, not a
heuristic.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[No shorter window]\label{cor:noshorter}
Two order-$L$ registers with different output streams already differ at some
index $<2L$; the observation window cannot be shortened below $2L$ without
ambiguity.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Sharpness}

Over $\GF(2)$ with $L=3$ take
\[
  x=0\,0\,1\,0\,0\,0\,0\,0\dots,\qquad
  y=0\,0\,1\,0\,0\,1\,0\,0\,1\dots
\]
Both have complexity at most $3$: $x$ comes from the zero tap vector with seed
$(0,0,1)$, and $y$ is $3$-periodic, hence generated by the recirculating taps
$u^{(3)}$ with the same seed (Theorem~\ref{thm:periodic}). They agree on indices
$0,\dots,4$, i.e.\ on $2L-1=5$ symbols, and differ at index $5$. An exhaustive
scan of the $4^{3}=64$ binary order-$3$ parameter pairs confirms both halves:
grouping outputs by their first $2L=6$ symbols gives $43$ groups, each containing
a single stream, whereas grouping by the first $5$ symbols produces groups with
distinct streams.

\section{Well-posedness: when are the taps unique?}\label{sec:unique}

Theorem~\ref{thm:2L} identifies the \emph{stream}, not the \emph{register}: by
Proposition~\ref{prop:zeroseed} the all-zero stream is emitted by every order-$L$
register, so ``the taps are determined by the stream'' is false as stated. The
correct statement is a criterion. Assume $F$ is a field.

\begin{definition}[Window span]
$W(x,L)=\Span_F\{\,w_n(x):n\in\NN\,\}\le F^{L}$, the row space of the infinite
Hankel matrix $H_{n,i}=x_{n+i}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness from spanning]\label{thm:uniq1}
If $W(x,L)=F^{L}$ and both $c$ and $d$ generate $x$ at order $L$, then $c=d$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $e=c-d$ with associated functional $\langle e,v\rangle=\sum_{i<L}e_iv_i$.
Subtracting the two recurrences at time $n$,
\[
  \langle e, w_n(x)\rangle=\sum_{i<L}c_ix_{n+i}-\sum_{i<L}d_ix_{n+i}
  =x_{n+L}-x_{n+L}=0 .
\]
So the functional vanishes on every window, hence on their span $F^{L}$; testing
against the standard basis gives $e=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Finite certificate]
It suffices that $w_0(x),\dots,w_{L-1}(x)$ span $F^{L}$---a rank condition
testable on the first $2L-1$ observed symbols.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Spanning from uniqueness]\label{thm:uniq2}
If $x$ is generated at order $L$ by taps $c$ and $c$ is the \emph{only} such tap
vector, then $W(x,L)=F^{L}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Contrapositive. If $W(x,L)\neq F^{L}$, then, being a proper subspace of a
finite-dimensional space, it is contained in the kernel of some nonzero
functional $\varphi$. Writing $\varphi(v)=\sum_{i<L}e_iv_i$ with
$e_i=\varphi(\delta_i)$ and $e\neq0$, we get $\sum_{i<L}e_ix_{n+i}=0$ for all
$n$. Adding this identity to the recurrence shows that $c+e$ also generates $x$;
since $e\neq0$ this is a second tap vector, contradicting uniqueness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Characterization]\label{thm:uniqchar}
For a stream $x$ generated at order $L$ by taps $c$: the tap vector is unique if
and only if $W(x,L)=F^{L}$.
\end{theorem}

Quantitatively over $\GF(2)$, the consistent tap vectors form an affine subspace
of dimension $L-\operatorname{rank}$, so exactly $2^{L-\operatorname{rank}}$ of
them fit the data. Exhaustive verification at $L=4$ confirms this: a
maximal-length register gives rank $4$ and a unique tap vector; the all-zero
stream gives rank $0$ and all $16$ tap vectors; a period-$2$ stream embedded at
order $4$ gives rank $4$ and a unique answer.

\begin{remark}
The naive conjecture ``tap recovery is unique'' is false, and the repair was not
a stronger proof but a better definition. By Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqchar} the
Hankel spanning condition is not an extra hypothesis inserted to save a theorem:
it is logically equivalent to the desired conclusion.
\end{remark}

\section{Linear congruential generators: one detector, two
families}\label{sec:lcg}

\begin{definition}
Over a commutative ring $R$, the LCG with multiplier $a$, increment $b$ and seed
$s$ emits $\lcg(a,b,s)_0=s$ and $\lcg(a,b,s)_{n+1}=a\,\lcg(a,b,s)_n+b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Family collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
Every LCG stream obeys the order-$2$ linear recurrence with tap vector
$(-a,\;1+a)$:
\[
  x_{n+2}=(1+a)\,x_{n+1}-a\,x_n .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $x_{n+2}=ax_{n+1}+b$ and $x_{n+1}=ax_n+b$, subtraction gives
$x_{n+2}-x_{n+1}=a(x_{n+1}-x_n)$, i.e.\ the stated identity.
\end{proof}

Hence Sections~\ref{sec:replay}--\ref{sec:unique} apply verbatim with $L=2$:
four observations identify the stream, replay is exact, and well-posedness is a
$2\times2$ rank condition. No second detector and no second correctness proof are
required.

\begin{theorem}[Recurrence implies exact replay]
If $x_{n+1}=ax_n+b$ for all $n$, then $x=\lcg(a,b,x_0)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$; the base case is definitional and the step applies the
recursion to both sides.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Seed recovery, backward and forward}

\begin{definition}
If $a$ is invertible in $R$, the \emph{inverse step} is
$\unstep(y)=a^{-1}(y-b)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Backward recovery]\label{thm:backward}
If $a^{-1}a=1$ then $\unstep$ is a two-sided inverse of $x\mapsto ax+b$, that map
is a bijection of $R$, and for all $n$,
$\unstep^{\,n}\bigl(\lcg(a,b,s)_n\bigr)=s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\unstep(ax+b)=a^{-1}(ax)=x$ and $a\unstep(y)+b=(y-b)+b=y$, so the map is
bijective. The displayed identity follows by induction on $n$, each step peeling
off one application.
\end{proof}

This is exact and search-free. Over $\mathbb{Z}/m$ with $\gcd(a,m)=1$ the
inverse is computed by the extended Euclidean algorithm in $O(\log m)$ steps.

\begin{theorem}[Pure periodicity]\label{thm:period}
Let $R$ be finite and $a$ invertible. Then the step map is a permutation of $R$,
so it has finite order $p>0$ with $(x\mapsto ax+b)^{p}=\mathrm{id}$; hence
$\lcg(a,b,s)_{n+p}=\lcg(a,b,s)_n$ for all $n$ and $\lcg(a,b,s)_{kp}=s$ for all
$k$. The orbit is \emph{purely} periodic: there is no transient prefix.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Bijectivity is Theorem~\ref{thm:backward}; a bijection of a finite set is a
permutation and has finite order in the symmetric group, so some positive
iterate is the identity. The stream is the orbit of $s$, so shifting the index by
$p$ leaves it unchanged; iterating from $n=0$ gives the multiple-of-$p$
statement.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Forward recovery]\label{cor:forward}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:period}, for any observed state
$\lcg(a,b,s)_n$ there is $k\ge0$ with
$\lcg\bigl(a,b,\lcg(a,b,s)_n\bigr)_k=s$; explicitly $k=np-n$ works. The seed is
reachable by running the generator \emph{forward}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Restarting the generator at a later state continues the same stream, so the
displayed value equals $\lcg(a,b,s)_{n+k}$; with $k=np-n$ (valid since $n\le np$
for $p\ge1$) this is $\lcg(a,b,s)_{np}=s$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:period}.
\end{proof}

An adversary or compressor equipped only with the forward generator---no modular
inverse, no algebraic solver---can still recover the seed. Rewinding is a special
case of fast-forwarding.

\subsection{Congruential data is rare}

\begin{theorem}[Three parameters]\label{thm:lcgcount}
Over $\mathbb{Z}/m$ the set of length-$N$ prefixes producible by some LCG has at
most $m^{3}$ elements, for every $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The prefix is the image of $(a,b,s)\in(\mathbb{Z}/m)^{3}$ under a fixed map, and
the image of a set of size $m^{3}$ has size at most $m^{3}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $m\ge2$ and $N>3$ then some length-$N$ stream over $\mathbb{Z}/m$ is produced
by no LCG, since $m^{3}<m^{N}$; a sound detector must reject it, and any
detector's false-positive budget is $m^{3-N}$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The census: how much data is seed-compressible?}\label{sec:census}

Fix the binary field and model a file as $w\in\{0,1\}^{N}$.

\begin{definition}[$L$-seed compressibility]
$w$ is \emph{$L$-seed compressible} if there are binary taps $c$ and seed $s$ of
length $L$ with $w_i=R(c,s)_i$ for every $i<N$, the register running over
$\GF(2)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The seed decoder]
$D_{N,L}$ reads a program $p\in\{0,1\}^{*}$, interprets its first $L$ bits as
taps and its next $L$ bits as a seed (missing bits default to $0$), runs the
register, and outputs the first $N$ symbols.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The falsifiability gate]\label{thm:gate}
$w$ is $L$-seed compressible if and only if some program $p$ satisfies
$D_{N,L}(p)=w$ exactly. The witnessing program is the concatenation of the tap
bits and the seed bits, of length exactly $2L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) The concatenated program is read back correctly by the decoder's
indexing, so $D_{N,L}(p)$ is the register output, which agrees with $w$ on the
first $N$ positions. ($\Leftarrow$) Extract the first $L$ and next $L$ bits of
$p$ as $(c,s)$; unfolding the decoder shows $w$ is the corresponding register
output.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Description complexity]
Writing $\KC_D(w)$ for the length of the shortest program $p$ with $D(p)=w$: if
$w$ is $L$-seed compressible then $\KC_{D_{N,L}}(w)\le 2L$, independently of $N$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Upper bounds on the population}

\begin{theorem}[Parameter bound]\label{thm:paramcount}
For all $N,L$, at most $4^{L}$ files of length $N$ are $L$-seed compressible.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each such file is the image of a pair (taps, seed) under the map sending
parameters to the length-$N$ prefix; there are $2^{L}\cdot2^{L}=4^{L}$ pairs.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The parameter bound is never tight]\label{thm:sharp}
For all $N,L$, at most $4^{L}-2^{L}+1$ files of length $N$ are $L$-seed
compressible.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Split the parameter pairs into the $2^{L}$ zero-seed pairs and the rest. By
Proposition~\ref{prop:zeroseed} all zero-seed pairs map to the single all-zero
file, so the image lies in a set of size at most $1+(4^{L}-2^{L})$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential rarity]\label{thm:rare}
If $2L\le N$, at most $2^{2L}$ files of length $N$ are $L$-seed compressible: a
fraction $2^{2L-N}$ of all $2^{N}$ files.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Non-vacuity]
If $2L<N$ then some length-$N$ file is not $L$-seed compressible.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The exact census}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:2L} two distinct streams of complexity at most $L$ differ
within the first $2L$ symbols, so counting distinct length-$2L$ prefixes counts
distinct \emph{infinite} streams exactly. Exhaustive enumeration over $\GF(2)$
gives:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrr}
\toprule
$L$ & distinct streams & $\tfrac13(2\cdot4^{L}+1)$ & bound $4^{L}-2^{L}+1$ & naive $4^{L}$\\
\midrule
1 & 3 & 3 & 3 & 4\\
2 & 11 & 11 & 13 & 16\\
3 & 43 & 43 & 57 & 64\\
4 & 171 & 171 & 241 & 256\\
5 & 683 & 683 & 993 & 1024\\
6 & 2731 & 2731 & 4033 & 4096\\
7 & 10923 & 10923 & 16257 & 16384\\
8 & 43691 & 43691 & 65281 & 65536\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{conjecture}[Exact census]\label{conj:census}
The number of distinct binary streams of linear complexity at most $L$ is
exactly $\tfrac13\bigl(2\cdot4^{L}+1\bigr)$; equivalently, the realizable
fraction of the $4^{L}$ parameter pairs tends to $2/3$.
\end{conjecture}

The conjecture is supported by exhaustive computation for $L\le8$ and is not
proved here. Structurally, the map (taps, seed) $\mapsto$ stream factors through
the pair (minimal connection polynomial, residue class), so the fibre over a
stream consists of the registers whose characteristic polynomial is a multiple of
the minimal one---a count of monic cofactors of the appropriate degree.
Note that the census refutes the naive expectation of a $\Theta(2^{L})$ count:
the realizable streams have positive density in parameter space, so the count is
$\Theta(4^{L})$ and the improvement over Theorem~\ref{thm:paramcount} is a
constant factor.

\subsection{The router dichotomy}

The practical proposal is a \emph{router}: classify each file as either
seed-compressible (pay $2L$ bits) or model-compressible (hand it to a fixed
decompressor $D$ and demand $\KC_D(w)+d\le N$). The two boxes cannot cover file
space. The model branch is limited by the counting bound behind every
incompressibility theorem: for fixed $D$,
$2^{d}\,\#\{w:\KC_D(w)+d\le N\}\le 2^{N+1}$.

\begin{theorem}[Router dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $D:\{0,1\}^{*}\to\{0,1\}^{N}$ be surjective and let $L,d\ge0$ satisfy
\[
  2^{d}\,2^{2L}+2^{N+1}\;<\;2^{d}\,2^{N}.
\]
Then some file $w\in\{0,1\}^{N}$ is neither $L$-seed compressible nor $d$-bit
compressible under $D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $A$ be the set of $L$-seed compressible files, so $|A|\le2^{2L}$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:rare}, and $B=\{w:\KC_D(w)+d\le N\}$, so $2^{d}|B|\le2^{N+1}$.
Then $2^{d}(|A|+|B|)\le 2^{d}2^{2L}+2^{N+1}<2^{d}2^{N}$, whence
$|A|+|B|<2^{N}$ and $|A\cup B|<|\{0,1\}^{N}|$; any file outside $A\cup B$ works.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Concrete instance]
Among $64$-bit files, whatever decompressor is installed on the model branch,
some file is neither the output of an order-$8$ register (a $16$-bit seed) nor
compressible by $4$ bits: the hypothesis reads
$2^{4}2^{16}+2^{65}<2^{4}2^{64}$, which holds.
\end{corollary}

Detection changes \emph{which} files sit on the compressible side of the
pigeonhole bound, not \emph{how many}. Its value is that the files it moves are
statistically flat yet computationally trivial---exactly those every statistical
compressor misses.

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algorithms}

\subsection{Berlekamp--Massey (identification)}
Given $s_0,\dots,s_{n-1}$ over a field, the algorithm returns the minimal $L$ and
a connection polynomial $C(X)=1+C_1X+\dots+C_LX^{L}$ with
$s_j=\sum_{i=1}^{L}C_is_{j-i}$ for $j\ge L$. It maintains a current candidate
$C$, the previous candidate $B$ from the last length change, and a discrepancy;
a nonzero discrepancy corrects $C$ by a shifted multiple of $B$, and the register
length grows when necessary. Cost: $O(n^{2})$ field operations, $O(n)$ storage.
Its correctness as a \emph{detector} is Corollary~\ref{cor:sound}. The paper's
tap convention is recovered by reversal: $c_i=C_{L-i}$.

\subsection{Detection and recovery pipeline}
Given a file $w$ of length $N$ and a maximum order $L_{\max}\le N/2$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Run Berlekamp--Massey on the first $2L_{\max}$ symbols to obtain $L$ and
  taps $c$.
\item If $2L\ge N$, reject: no compression gain.
\item Replay $R(c,(w_0,\dots,w_{L-1}))$ for $N$ steps.
\item \textbf{Gate:} compare with $w$ bit for bit; on any mismatch reject and
  route to the model branch, otherwise emit the $2L$-bit program.
\item Optionally test the congruential signature: if $L=2$ with taps $(-a,1+a)$,
  report multiplier $a$, increment $b=w_1-aw_0$ and seed $w_0$, and verify by
  replay.
\end{enumerate}
The gate makes false acceptance impossible; steps 1 and 3 cost $O(L^{2})$ and
$O(NL)$.

\subsection{Well-posedness test}
Assemble the Hankel matrix of windows and compute its rank $r$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqchar} the taps are unique iff $r=L$; over $\GF(2)$ exactly
$2^{L-r}$ tap vectors are consistent. Cost $O(L^{3})$ field operations.

\subsection{Congruential seed recovery}
With state $x_n$, known $(a,b,m)$ and $\gcd(a,m)=1$: compute $a^{-1}$ and apply
$y\mapsto a^{-1}(y-b)$ exactly $n$ times (Theorem~\ref{thm:backward}); or avoid
inversion by running forward until the state repeats
(Corollary~\ref{cor:forward}). Backward recovery costs $O(n)$ multiplications, or
$O(\log n)$ using $x_n=a^{n}s+b\frac{a^{n}-1}{a-1}$.

\section{Applications}

\textbf{Archival of procedurally generated assets.} Terrain, foliage placement,
noise textures and dungeon layouts are generated from a seed and then persisted;
when the generator is a linear recurrence or a congruential map, the asset
compresses to its seed with the exact-replay guarantee.

\textbf{Simulation reproducibility.} Monte Carlo studies persist random streams
for exact re-runs; such streams are seed compressible by construction and the
gate certifies bit-exactness.

\textbf{Cryptanalytic hygiene.} Theorem~\ref{thm:2L} is the classical warning
against bare shift-register keystreams: $2L$ known symbols recover the register
and hence the whole keystream. Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqchar} sharpens this by saying
exactly when the recovered register is the true one.

\textbf{Corpus triage.} The router is deployable---detect, gate, compress or fall
through---and Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} sets expectations honestly, while
Theorem~\ref{thm:periodic} identifies a real corpus that is caught: periodic and
tiled data.

\textbf{Scrambled transport streams.} Ethernet, SONET and satellite links
scramble payloads by XOR-ing with register output; with known plaintext the same
machinery strips the scrambler.

\section{Discussion and future work}\label{sec:discussion}

Three themes emerge. \emph{First, the change of language does the work}:
recasting streams as a module over the polynomial ring, with the shift operator
as the indeterminate, converts ``obeys a recurrence'' into ``is annihilated by a
polynomial''; subadditivity is then the statement that annihilators multiply and
the $2L$ bound is degree arithmetic. \emph{Second, degeneracy is data}: the
failed conjecture about tap uniqueness was repaired by identifying the invariant
that governs the phenomenon---the Hankel rank---and the repair is an exact
characterization. \emph{Third, counting is unforgiving}: every statement about
what detection achieves is bracketed by a statement about how little of the
universe it reaches.

\paragraph{C1. The exact census.} Prove Conjecture~\ref{conj:census}. The map
(taps, seed) $\mapsto$ stream factors through (minimal connection polynomial,
residue class), so the fibre over a stream is the set of registers whose
characteristic polynomial is a multiple of the minimal one, i.e.\ the monic
cofactors of the appropriate degree. The polynomial--register dictionary
(Theorems~\ref{thm:annihilation} and \ref{thm:realize}) is in place; what remains
is divisibility bookkeeping and the degenerate strata.

\paragraph{C2. Minimal complexity and sharpness.} Prove that for every $L$ there
exist streams of complexity exactly $L$ agreeing on the first $2L-1$ symbols and
differing at index $2L-1$, upgrading the concrete witnesses above to a uniform
family; and prove that Berlekamp--Massey computes $\lc$ exactly, closing the loop
between algorithm and invariant.

\paragraph{C3. Non-linear families.} Extend detection to tempered linear
generators of Mersenne-Twister type (linear over $\GF(2)$ in a high-dimensional
state, hence within reach at large $L$), permuted congruential generators, and
counter-based constructions; each needs an enlarged linear model or solver-based
inversion, and its own well-posedness criterion.

\paragraph{C4. Robust detection under corruption.} Real files interleave
generator output with headers, checksums and edits. The natural generalization is
approximate linear complexity: the least $L$ such that some order-$L$ register
agrees with the data outside $k$ positions. With an error budget in the gate this
extends the router to partially seed-compressible files while keeping bit-exact
verification (store the seed plus the corrections).

\paragraph{C5. Quantifying real corpora.} Instrument the pipeline over public
corpora and measure what fraction of real data is seed compressible at practical
orders. Theorem~\ref{thm:rare} predicts a negligible fraction for random files;
the empirical question is how much structured real data---padding, tiles,
scrambled regions---is caught, and at what order.

